WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.157
Choosing the base model: size, cost, and license.

2
00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:05.592
Three constraints narrow the choice quickly.

3
00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:14.469
Size: small enough to fine-tune with LoRA or QLoRA on the GPU you can get (week 3) and to serve cheaply (lesson 11).

4
00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:21.384
For an extraction task, models in the roughly 1 to 8 billion parameter range are the usual starting point.

5
00:00:21.664 --> 00:00:31.200
Fit: an instruction-tuned variant with a chat template, a context length that covers your longest input, and support in the serving engine you plan to use.

6
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:37.155
License: the terms that govern what you can do with an adapter built on it, and whether you can publish it.

7
00:00:37.435 --> 00:00:42.704
Check the license on the exact model card, because sizes within one family can differ.

8
00:00:42.984 --> 00:00:51.560
At this lesson’s review date, the Hugging Face card for Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 lists Apache 2.

9
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.037
0, while the card for Qwen2.

10
00:00:54.317 --> 00:01:01.592
5-3B-Instruct lists the Qwen Research License, a more restrictive license, even though the neighboring 1.

11
00:01:01.872 --> 00:01:04.731
5B model in that family is Apache 2.

12
00:01:05.011 --> 00:01:05.779
0.

13
00:01:06.059 --> 00:01:18.048
Meta’s Llama models are gated and distributed under Llama community licenses, which set conditions on derivative models; read the specific license before you publish anything derived from one.

14
00:01:18.328 --> 00:01:21.315
An adapter is not independent of its base model.

15
00:01:21.595 --> 00:01:28.272
It is useless without the base weights and is derived from them, so the base model’s license governs what you may do with it.

16
00:01:28.552 --> 00:01:38.301
Record in your plan the license name, a link, any attribution or naming requirements, and any acceptable-use policy, because they all go into your model card.

17
00:01:38.581 --> 00:01:49.675
If you also use a frontier model to generate or label training data, read that provider’s terms on using outputs for training (lesson 12), and note your conclusion in the plan.
